<Window x:Class="StickyDock.App.Views.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:behaviors="clr-namespace:StickyDock.App.Docking"
        xmlns:input="clr-namespace:StickyDock.App.Input"
        xmlns:coreDocking="clr-namespace:StickyDock.Core.Docking;assembly=StickyDock.Core"
        xmlns:coreServices="clr-namespace:StickyDock.Core.Services;assembly=StickyDock.Core"
        xmlns:interaction="clr-namespace:StickyDock.Core.Interaction;assembly=StickyDock.Core"
        Title="Sticky Dock Utility"
        Width="120"
        Height="36"
        WindowStyle="None"
        AllowsTransparency="True"
        Background="Transparent"
        Topmost="True"
        ResizeMode="NoResize"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        WindowStartupLocation="Manual">
    <!--
        ADR-002 / ADR-003. The window is entirely client area (CaptionHeight = 0) so that the pointer events of E3 keep
        arriving; the drag is delegated to the native move loop through the DragCommand of the view model, triggered on
        the press because a left-click gesture fires on the release. The resize border and the glass frame are removed so
        that no invisible band steals clicks from neighbouring windows.

        The Width and Height above are only the size the window has before the docking coordinator applies the declared
        footprint of DockBarMetrics; from then on the size is whatever the coordinator sets with SetWindowPos.
    -->
    <WindowChrome.WindowChrome>
        <WindowChrome CaptionHeight="0"
                      ResizeBorderThickness="0"
                      GlassFrameThickness="0"
                      CornerRadius="0"
                      UseAeroCaptionButtons="False" />
    </WindowChrome.WindowChrome>

    <Window.Resources>
        <!--
            Deployed surface. The slide and the fade are storyboards with literal values: Timeline.From and
            Timeline.Duration are not dependency properties, so the animation cannot receive the offset or the duration
            by binding (ADR-003, P5.b). The magnitude is the thickness gained when deploying, 96 - 36 = 60 units, and the
            duration is InteractionTimings.SlideMilliseconds, 0.16 s (deuda D-14, verified by M-05).

            FillBehavior is HoldEnd in every storyboard. With Stop, the clock of a finished animation is removed and the
            previous fade (held at zero opacity by the collapse) would become effective again, leaving the panel
            invisible on the next deployment.
        -->
        <Style TargetType="{x:Type Border}" x:Key="ContentPanelStyle">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{Binding TabBackground}" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <!-- Nothing is deployed while the bar is collapsed or arming, so the surface is not even laid out. -->
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding DockState}" Value="{x:Static coreServices:DockState.Tab}">
                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                </DataTrigger>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding DockState}" Value="{x:Static coreServices:DockState.Arming}">
                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                </DataTrigger>

                <!-- Deployed against the top edge: the content enters from beyond the top of the screen. -->
                <MultiDataTrigger>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding DockState}" Value="{x:Static coreServices:DockState.Expanded}" />
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding DockEdge}" Value="{x:Static coreDocking:DockEdge.Top}" />
                    </MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.EnterActions>
                        <BeginStoryboard>
                            <Storyboard FillBehavior="HoldEnd">
                                <DoubleAnimation
                                    Storyboard.TargetProperty="(UIElement.RenderTransform).(TranslateTransform.Y)"
                                    From="-60" To="0" Duration="0:0:0.16" />
                                <DoubleAnimation Storyboard.TargetProperty="Opacity"
                                                 From="0.3" To="1" Duration="0:0:0.16" />
                            </Storyboard>
                        </BeginStoryboard>
                    </MultiDataTrigger.EnterActions>
                </MultiDataTrigger>

                <!-- Deployed against the bottom edge: the content enters from below. -->
                <MultiDataTrigger>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding DockState}" Value="{x:Static coreServices:DockState.Expanded}" />
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding DockEdge}" Value="{x:Static coreDocking:DockEdge.Bottom}" />
                    </MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.EnterActions>
                        <BeginStoryboard>
                            <Storyboard FillBehavior="HoldEnd">
                                <DoubleAnimation
                                    Storyboard.TargetProperty="(UIElement.RenderTransform).(TranslateTransform.Y)"
                                    From="60" To="0" Duration="0:0:0.16" />
                                <DoubleAnimation Storyboard.TargetProperty="Opacity"
                                                 From="0.3" To="1" Duration="0:0:0.16" />
                            </Storyboard>
                        </BeginStoryboard>
                    </MultiDataTrigger.EnterActions>
                </MultiDataTrigger>

                <!-- Deployed against the left edge: the content enters from the left. -->
                <MultiDataTrigger>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding DockState}" Value="{x:Static coreServices:DockState.Expanded}" />
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding DockEdge}" Value="{x:Static coreDocking:DockEdge.Left}" />
                    </MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.EnterActions>
                        <BeginStoryboard>
                            <Storyboard FillBehavior="HoldEnd">
                                <DoubleAnimation
                                    Storyboard.TargetProperty="(UIElement.RenderTransform).(TranslateTransform.X)"
                                    From="-60" To="0" Duration="0:0:0.16" />
                                <DoubleAnimation Storyboard.TargetProperty="Opacity"
                                                 From="0.3" To="1" Duration="0:0:0.16" />
                            </Storyboard>
                        </BeginStoryboard>
                    </MultiDataTrigger.EnterActions>
                </MultiDataTrigger>

                <!-- Deployed against the right edge: the content enters from the right. -->
                <MultiDataTrigger>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding DockState}" Value="{x:Static coreServices:DockState.Expanded}" />
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding DockEdge}" Value="{x:Static coreDocking:DockEdge.Right}" />
                    </MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.EnterActions>
                        <BeginStoryboard>
                            <Storyboard FillBehavior="HoldEnd">
                                <DoubleAnimation
                                    Storyboard.TargetProperty="(UIElement.RenderTransform).(TranslateTransform.X)"
                                    From="60" To="0" Duration="0:0:0.16" />
                                <DoubleAnimation Storyboard.TargetProperty="Opacity"
                                                 From="0.3" To="1" Duration="0:0:0.16" />
                            </Storyboard>
                        </BeginStoryboard>
                    </MultiDataTrigger.EnterActions>
                </MultiDataTrigger>

                <!-- Floating bar: it has no edge to come from, so it only fades in (ADR-003, §12 #9). -->
                <MultiDataTrigger>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding DockState}" Value="{x:Static coreServices:DockState.Expanded}" />
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding DockEdge}" Value="{x:Null}" />
                    </MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.EnterActions>
                        <BeginStoryboard>
                            <Storyboard FillBehavior="HoldEnd">
                                <DoubleAnimation Storyboard.TargetProperty="Opacity"
                                                 From="0.3" To="1" Duration="0:0:0.16" />
                            </Storyboard>
                        </BeginStoryboard>
                    </MultiDataTrigger.EnterActions>
                </MultiDataTrigger>

                <!-- Slide-out: a fade, because the specified animation is the deployment (§4.3-01, §12 #11). -->
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding DockState}" Value="{x:Static coreServices:DockState.Collapsing}">
                    <DataTrigger.EnterActions>
                        <BeginStoryboard>
                            <Storyboard FillBehavior="HoldEnd">
                                <DoubleAnimation Storyboard.TargetProperty="Opacity"
                                                 From="1" To="0" Duration="0:0:0.12" />
                            </Storyboard>
                        </BeginStoryboard>
                    </DataTrigger.EnterActions>
                </DataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>

        <!--
            Idle tab: the only drag handle and the visual anchor in both states (ADR-003, D3 and invariant I3). It keeps
            its position on screen while the bar deploys, so it carries no translation and no margin.

            The tab is rotated on the vertical edges because its content-sized footprint (about 58x27 units) does not fit
            the 36-unit thickness that DockBarMetrics declares for those edges.
        -->
        <Style TargetType="{x:Type Border}" x:Key="TabBorderStyle">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{Binding TabBackground}" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,6" />
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.9" />
            <Setter Property="IsHitTestVisible" Value="True" />
            <Setter Property="RenderTransformOrigin" Value="0.5,0.5" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding DockEdge}" Value="{x:Static coreDocking:DockEdge.Bottom}">
                    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
                    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Bottom" />
                </DataTrigger>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding DockEdge}" Value="{x:Static coreDocking:DockEdge.Left}">
                    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
                    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
                    <Setter Property="LayoutTransform">
                        <Setter.Value>
                            <RotateTransform Angle="90" />
                        </Setter.Value>
                    </Setter>
                </DataTrigger>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding DockEdge}" Value="{x:Static coreDocking:DockEdge.Right}">
                    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right" />
                    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
                    <Setter Property="LayoutTransform">
                        <Setter.Value>
                            <RotateTransform Angle="270" />
                        </Setter.Value>
                    </Setter>
                </DataTrigger>

                <!-- Micro-expansion of HU02 CA #2: 1.06 of the tab during ~120 ms, before the bar is fully deployed. -->
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding DockState}" Value="{x:Static coreServices:DockState.Arming}">
                    <DataTrigger.EnterActions>
                        <BeginStoryboard>
                            <Storyboard FillBehavior="HoldEnd">
                                <DoubleAnimation
                                    Storyboard.TargetProperty="(UIElement.RenderTransform).(ScaleTransform.ScaleX)"
                                    To="1.06" Duration="0:0:0.06" AutoReverse="True" />
                                <DoubleAnimation
                                    Storyboard.TargetProperty="(UIElement.RenderTransform).(ScaleTransform.ScaleY)"
                                    To="1.06" Duration="0:0:0.06" AutoReverse="True" />
                            </Storyboard>
                        </BeginStoryboard>
                    </DataTrigger.EnterActions>
                </DataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>
    </Window.Resources>

    <!--
        The root does not clip: clipping here would cut the micro-expansion of the tab against the window border.

        It reports the Bar region because the interactive area (A4) is the union of the deployed bar and its ribbons, and
        the window footprint covers exactly that area in every state. Its own Background makes it hit-testable across the
        whole client area, which ContentPanel cannot be: ContentPanel is translated and clipped while the slide runs.

        The region is not bound to the entry of a child element, because there is no such entry to observe: the root is
        the ancestor of both the panel and the tab, so the pointer is already inside it while it hovers the tab, and no
        MouseEnter announces the move into the panel. WindowRegionReporter reaffirms the region on every pointer move and
        reconciles it with the real cursor after a change of size or position, which covers the pointer that does not move
        while the bar grows under it (defect D-E3-01 of E3).

        Collateral, accepted and harmless: while the bar is collapsed the window measures exactly the tab footprint, so
        entering it reports Bar as well as Tab. In Tab the machine only reads Tab, so nothing activates (E1-CA4). In
        Arming, leaving the tab while still inside that footprint settles straight to Expanded (ADR-003, §7.2 "Leave(Tab)
        with another active region"): the pointer is already inside the bar area, and the footprint did not grow.
    -->
    <Grid x:Name="RootPanel"
          Background="Transparent"
          input:WindowRegionReporter.Region="{x:Static interaction:DockRegion.Bar}"
          input:WindowRegionReporter.EnterCommand="{Binding EnterRegionCommand}"
          input:WindowRegionReporter.LeaveCommand="{Binding LeaveRegionCommand}">
        <Grid x:Name="ClipPanel" ClipToBounds="True">
            <Border x:Name="ContentPanel" Style="{StaticResource ContentPanelStyle}">
                <Border.RenderTransform>
                    <TranslateTransform X="0" Y="0" />
                </Border.RenderTransform>
            </Border>
        </Grid>

        <Border x:Name="TabBorder"
                Panel.ZIndex="1"
                Style="{StaticResource TabBorderStyle}"
                behaviors:PressDragBehavior.Command="{Binding DragCommand}"
                input:PointerRegionBehavior.Region="{x:Static interaction:DockRegion.Tab}"
                input:PointerRegionBehavior.EnterCommand="{Binding EnterRegionCommand}"
                input:PointerRegionBehavior.LeaveCommand="{Binding LeaveRegionCommand}">
            <Border.RenderTransform>
                <ScaleTransform ScaleX="1" ScaleY="1" />
            </Border.RenderTransform>
            <TextBlock Text="Sticky" Foreground="{Binding TabForeground}" FontSize="11" />
        </Border>
    </Grid>
</Window>
